    <origin>Owner decision round 2, item 9 (2026-09-30): "target Created By and Assigned To. A team-owned record does not fan out to team members." Q8: the to-do "for" person is the existing sprk_todo.sprk_assignedto (CONTACT lookup) plus Created By. The #1044 split agreed with word-add-in-r1 on 2026-09-30 (session27 notes, "Peer follow-through"): UAC 152 matches Assigned To to the user's contact, counts Created By only for a HUMAN creator, and makes the server generators set Assigned To; word-add-in-r1 task 083 owns the Office CreateTodoAsync default only. Session 27 notifications research. GitHub #1044 (filed by word-add-in-r1; the membership-widening half stays with UAC).</origin>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>Make every Daily Briefing channel, the Workspace top-priority-matter briefing, and the LookupUserMembership notification path select records by the PEOPLE named on them. Those people are the human creator (Created By), the caller's own user ownership, and the "Assigned *" contact columns matched through the caller's linked contact from task 141. Never select by owner team, business unit or organization. Make the briefing show only rows the caller can read. Make the server-side to-do writers UAC owns fill Assigned To, so a server-created to-do reaches a person. Make the four MembershipChangedEvent publishers, and the reconciliation backstop, describe the row's actual owner instead of asserting "the caller owns it".</prompt>

  <role>SPAARKE platform developer on the ADR-034 membership resolver, the Daily Briefing collector and the notification spine. Expert in Dataverse ownership semantics (polymorphic ownerid, default owner teams, app-only createdby) and in the repo's impersonated-read seam.</role>

  <goal>(1) IMembershipResolverService supports a PEOPLE-TARGETING consumption surface, selected by an explicit option. It binds only person terms: a human `createdby`, the user-valued owner, and registry Contact-typed "Assigned *" columns through the linked contact. It never binds `owningteam`, `owningbusinessunit`, or any Team-, BusinessUnit-, Organization- or Account-typed descriptor.
(2) These surfaces use it: DailyBriefingCollector (all six channels plus High Priority, render and email legs), BriefingService.GetTopPriorityMatterAsync, and every live notification playbook's LookupUserMembership node.
(3) Every briefing row the caller receives is one Dataverse lets the caller read, with no app-only fallback.
(4) TodoGenerationService, TaskActionCore and the external-portal to-do create set Assigned To when the request names no assignee. They use the triggering person's contact, else the parent's responsible internal contact.
(5) The MembershipChangedEvent publishers and MembershipReconciliationJob key each event on the row's real owner (team or user), in the same identity space.
(6) ADR-034 is amended (path B) to name the third surface and its human-createdby exception. docs/architecture/membership-resolution-pattern.md and the node-author guides match.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>WHAT IS WRONG TODAY (verified 2026-09-30 against this branch; anchors re-verified by the reviewer the same day).

1. BRIEFING TO-DOS VANISH WHEN TEAM-OWNED. `Services/Ai/Narrators/DailyBriefingCollector.cs:1027` filters `owninguser = systemUserId`, so a to-do owned by a team matches nobody. Server-created rows become team-owned once RecordOwnershipResolver is wired (see C10 and word-add-in-r1 task 080). The High Priority to-do spec does the same: `:427-432`, with `ScopeToOwner: true` threaded to `:502-505`. The collector's own docstring (`:987-989`) says it skipped `sprk_assignedto` because that column is a contact lookup and matching needed an identity round-trip. GitHub #1044 records this. The owner chose `sprk_todo.sprk_assignedto` (CONTACT, verified live) plus Created By.

2. MATTERS, PROJECTS AND EVENTS FAN OUT TO THE WHOLE BUSINESS UNIT. The collector resolves candidates with `_membershipResolver.ResolveAsync(systemUserId, entity, options: null, ct)` (`:627-629`).
   - With `options: null`, `AccessConferringOnly` is false and ALL discovered descriptors are used (`MembershipResolverService.cs:255-257`). That includes the platform ownership columns (`PlatformOwnershipColumns = { ownerid, owningteam, owningbusinessunit }`, `:595-596`).
   - `owningteam` binds every id in `identity.TeamIds` (`:904-909`). `IdentityNormalizationService` loads TeamIds from `teammembership` with no `isdefault` exclusion (`IdentityNormalizationService.cs:656-707`), so a BU DEFAULT team matches, and that team contains every user in the BU.
   - `owningbusinessunit` binds the caller's BU id (`:911-916`).
   - Result: a team- or BU-owned matter, project or event surfaces in the briefing of every user in that BU.
   - The same resolver call shape feeds two other surfaces. One is the Workspace briefing's top-priority matter (`Services/Workspace/BriefingService.cs:291-292`, `options: null`). The other is the `LookupUserMembership` playbook node (`Services/Ai/Nodes/LookupUserMembershipNodeExecutor.cs:261-265`, `Roles` only), which ADR-034 names as the notification playbooks' membership source.

3. HIGH PRIORITY IS AN ORG-WIDE APP-ONLY DISCLOSURE (found while tracing; added per the no-deferral directive).
   - `CollectHighPriorityAsync` (`DailyBriefingCollector.cs:337-383`) queries six entity types with `ScopeToOwner: false` (`:405-426`): matter, project, invoice, document, work assignment and event. It returns every flagged (`sprk_highpriority` or `sprk_monitor`) record of those types in the tenant, with names and descriptions, to every caller.
   - The seventh type, to-do, uses `owninguser`.
   - The reads go through the app-only `IGenericEntityService` (`:163`, `:507`).
   - The collector's own comment (`:321-325`) calls owner scoping "a follow-up refinement".
   - The same list also grounds `DailyBriefingSuggestionProducer` (`DailyBriefingCompositeService.cs:130-133`).

4. THE BRIEFING NEVER ASKS DATAVERSE WHETHER THE CALLER MAY READ A ROW.
   - Every channel query runs app-only (`:773`, `:842`, `:898`, `:953`, `:1032`).
   - Narrowing to people does not by itself guarantee readability. A creator or an assignee can lose read access. Two examples: a child re-owned into the Secure Record BU by the C10 work, or an internal user named in an Assigned field on a secure record with no share.
   - ADR-047's privacy invariant says a notification must carry nothing the recipient could not re-fetch through the auth-checked BFF.
   - A generic caller-context seam already exists. `IImpersonatedCommunicationQuery` (`Services/Communication/IImpersonatedCommunicationQuery.cs:21`) is a singleton, registered unconditionally (`Infrastructure/DI/CommunicationModule.cs:279`). It wraps `DataverseWebApiService.RetrieveMultipleImpersonatedAsync` (`src/server/shared/Spaarke.Dataverse/DataverseWebApiService.cs:1014`) with a fully generic (entitySet, odataQuery, callerSystemUserId) contract. `ExternalAccessModule.cs:187-193` records the decision NOT to declare a second impersonated-query interface.

5. MEMBERSHIP EVENTS MISSTATE OWNERSHIP AND USE THE WRONG IDENTITY SPACE (ADR-034 publishers are ours). Four publishers publish `PersonId = caller AAD oid, PersonIdType = User, SourceField = "ownerid", Role = "owner"` unconditionally:
   - `Api/DataverseDocumentsEndpoints.cs:60-77` (POST /api/v1/documents);
   - `Api/Events/EventEndpoints.cs:400-418` (POST /api/v1/events);
   - `Api/Office/OfficeEndpoints.cs:1931-1954` (quick-create matter);
   - `Services/Office/OfficeService.cs:940-956` (Office save).

   Three things are wrong:
   - (a) When the row is team-owned the event is false. Office save already passes `OwningTeamId` (`OfficeDocumentPersistence.cs:252-257`), and POST /api/v1/documents writes a client- or server-chosen owner team (#1043).
   - (b) The writers disagree on identity. The comment at `OfficeEndpoints.cs:1936-1941` claims the junction updater resolves the oid to a systemuserid. It does not: `MembershipJunctionUpdater.cs:122-133` writes `evt.PersonId` verbatim into the natural key. Meanwhile `MembershipReconciliationJob.cs:531-546` keys on the lookup VALUE (a systemuserid or teamid), so the two writers produce different junction keys for the same membership. The comments at `DataverseDocumentsEndpoints.cs:55` and `EventEndpoints.cs:393-394` ("defaulted by Dataverse to the OBO caller") are also false. Both creates run on app-only singletons (`IDocumentDataverseService`/`IDataverseService`, `GraphModule.cs:47,95`; `IEventDataverseService` = `DataverseWebApiService`, `GraphModule.cs:100`).
   - (c) The backstop has its own typing defect. `ReadLookupAsIdentity` (`MembershipReconciliationJob.cs:742-776`) types the id from `descriptor.IdentityType`, and a polymorphic Owner descriptor always binds as SystemUser (ADR-034 A1.1, `MembershipFieldDiscoveryService` OwnerAttributeTargets). A team-owned row's team id is therefore recorded as `PersonIdType = User`.

   The reconciliation orphan scan (`MembershipReconciliationJob.cs:412-419`) removes junction rows that are not in the expected set. Oid-keyed rows written by the old publishers are therefore removed once both writers agree.

6. NOTIFICATIONS THEMSELVES.
   - `NotificationService.CreateNotificationAsync` (`Services/NotificationService.cs:55-81`) writes one `appnotification` per explicit systemuser (`ownerid = systemuser(userId)`).
   - Nothing notifies on document or to-do creation, and no producer expands a team into its members. `CommunicationFanOutTargetingService` targets message participants only.
   - So "a team-owned record notifies nobody" is true for the direct producers. The fan-out risk is the membership-driven path in item 2: LookupUserMembership feeding CreateNotification in per-user playbooks.
   - The owner's round-1 direction is that the Daily Briefing, not a team fan-out, is the surface for team-owned records.

7. SERVER-CREATED TO-DOS NAME NO PERSON (the #1044 split agreed with word-add-in-r1, session27 "Peer follow-through").
   - Created By is the BFF APPLICATION user for every BFF-created to-do. For example, "Invoice Pending: INV-005" has createdby = `# mi-bff-api-dev`. Matching Created By therefore cannot find the human, so the agreement makes Assigned To carry the person.
   - `TodoGenerationService.CreateTodoAsync` (`Services/Workspace/TodoGenerationService.cs:798`) writes no `sprk_assignedto`, and none of its five rule call sites (`:408`, `:483`, `:581`, `:655`, `:722`) passes an owner. Its to-dos are therefore app-owned, app-created and unassigned, and reach NO user's briefing today.
   - `TaskActionCore.CreateAsync` (`Services/Ai/Nodes/ActionCore/TaskActionCore.cs:102-158`) creates an `sprk_event` of type Task. It sets `ownerid` only from `input.OwnerId` (`:153-154`) and never sets `sprk_assignedto`, which is a registry Contact column on sprk_event.
   - The external-portal to-do create (`Infrastructure/ExternalAccess/ExternalDataService.cs:649`, payload built by `BuildTodoCreatePayload` at `:727`) writes no `sprk_assignedto`.
   - The Office `CreateTodoAsync` (`OfficeService.cs:2745`, assignee written at `:2788-2791`) is word-add-in-r1 task 083's. It is NOT this task's.

8. APP-ONLY CREATES ALSO LOSE THE HUMAN CREATOR FOR NON-TO-DO RECORDS. POST /api/v1/documents, POST /api/v1/events, Office save and quick-create matter all create app-only (item 5b). Their `createdby` is the application user, so the Created By term alone cannot surface them for the person who made them. Documents still reach that person through the parent-set path (a document on a matter or project the person is named on). Events and quick-created matters need a decision (see escalation).

THE OWNER'S RULE (binding): a record is "for" a person when that person CREATED it (a human createdby) or is NAMED in one of its "Assigned *" person columns. Team, business-unit and organization ownership are access facts, not attention facts. They keep granting ACCESS (ADR-034 A1.1, untouched here) but never select a record for someone's briefing or notification. Personal user ownership (`owninguser`) is kept as a third person term pending owner confirmation (escalation (a)).

THE USER-CONTACT LINK.
- The "Assigned *" columns are CONTACT lookups. The registry is `MembershipOptions.cs:300-372`, e.g. `sprk_todo.sprk_assignedto` at `:361-364`. `sprk_document` has no registry entry.
- Matching these columns to an internal caller needs the caller's contact. `IdentityNormalizationService.cs:139-150` uses `sprk_primarycontact`, else `contact.azureactivedirectoryobjectid`. That second column does not exist in dev (C7), and only 1 of 8 active interactive dev users has `sprk_primarycontact` set (session 27).
- Task 141 builds the reliable link (`systemuser.azureactivedirectoryobjectid` to `contact.sprk_externalobjectid`, then sets `sprk_primarycontact`), and `IdentityNormalizationService` exposes it as `PersonIdentity.ContactId`. This task consumes that and nothing else.</background>
